.TH TABS 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
tabs \- set terminal hardware tab stops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tabs
.RB [ \-N ]
.br
.B tabs
.B \-T
.I type
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tabs
writes an escape sequence to standard output that clears every
existing hardware tab stop on the terminal and sets new ones at a
uniform interval, then returns the cursor to column 1.
.PP
With no operands,
.B tabs
is equivalent to
.BR "tabs \-8" .
.PP
Tab stops are always set out to a fixed, generous column bound, since
this implementation has no way to query the attached terminal's real
width; a real terminal simply ignores a tab-stop request past its own
last column.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \- N
Set tab stops every
.I N
columns, where
.I N
is a single decimal digit from 1 to 9.
.TP
.B \-0
Refused: clearing the existing tab stops without setting any new ones
is not supported.
.TP
.BI \-T " type"
Refused unconditionally: this implementation has no terminal
capability database to look up
.I type
in, and only supports the uniform-interval form described above.
Note that no argument is consumed for
.BR \-T ,
so any following word is left to be processed as a separate,
also-unsupported operand.
.SH OPERANDS
None of the following forms of a real
.B tabs
utility are supported by this implementation, and each is refused
with a diagnostic rather than silently approximated:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a named preset such as
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-c2 ,
.BR \-c3 ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-s ,
or
.BR \-u ;
.IP \(bu 2
an explicit tab-stop list, such as
.RB \(dq "tabs 1,10 +8" \(dq.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The tab stops were set successfully.
.TP
.B >0
An unsupported option or operand was given, or a write to standard
output failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR expand (1),
.BR unexpand (1)
